  "articleBody": "Insulating a garage correctly makes a huge difference in comfort and energy bills, especially if you share walls with conditioned living areas. Avoid these frequent missteps:\n\n- Skipping air sealing before placing batts. Tucking insulation into drafty stud cavities without first caulking bottom plates and foaming electrical penetrations lets cold air pass right through.\n- Compressing insulation to fit tight cavities. Stuffing thick batts into shallow spaces or crushing them behind conduit reduces their thermal R-value. Insulation works through trapped air pockets, so it must fit smoothly without bunching.\n- Leaving voids in corners and behind boxes. Leaving hollow spaces behind junction boxes and in three-stud corners creates cold spots where condensation can form inside the wall.\n- Using insulation as an air barrier substitute. Fiberglass batts do not stop air movement; they only slow heat transfer. Relying on batts to plug drafty cracks leads to drafts and dust penetration.\n- Covering assemblies before required trade inspections. Hanging drywall over new insulation before plumbing, electrical, and insulation rough-ins are signed off by local inspectors can force costly teardowns.\n\nWhat to Do Next:\nTake time to inspect the framing, run your wiring, and seal every perimeter seam before unrolling insulation. For dependable garage insulation and interior finishing in the Denver area, call Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
